<Text-field selection-placeholder="false" executable="false" family="Times New Roman" opaque="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" superscript="false" placeholder="false" readonly="false" subscript="false" bold="false" italic="false" underline="false" background="[255,255,255]" size="12" linebreak="space" spaceabove="10" rightmargin="0" bullet="none" firstindent="0" bulletsuffix="" linespacing="0.0" pagebreak-before="false" leftmargin="0" alignment="left" initial="0" spacebelow="0"><Font selection-placeholder="false" executable="false" family="Times New Roman" opaque="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" superscript="false" placeholder="false" readonly="false" subscript="false" bold="false" italic="false" underline="false" background="[255,255,255]" size="12">Once the &quot;game&quot; is complete, the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence is equal to the number of piles.</Font></Text-field>
<Text-field selection-placeholder="false" executable="false" family="Times New Roman" opaque="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" superscript="false" placeholder="false" readonly="false" subscript="false" bold="false" italic="false" underline="false" background="[255,255,255]" size="12" linebreak="space" spaceabove="10" rightmargin="0" bullet="none" firstindent="0" bulletsuffix="" linespacing="0.0" pagebreak-before="false" leftmargin="0" alignment="left" initial="0" spacebelow="0"><Font selection-placeholder="false" executable="false" family="Times New Roman" opaque="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" superscript="false" placeholder="false" readonly="false" subscript="false" bold="false" italic="false" underline="false" background="[255,255,255]" size="12">Now that all of the cards are played, we obtain a strictly increasing subsequence by backtracking.  The top card on the final pile is 17, so 17 will be the last entry in the subsequence.  When 17 was placed on the pile, the top card on the pile before it was 16 (step 8), so 16 precedes 17 in the subsequence.  When 16 was placed on the third pile, the top card on the second pile was 11 (step 5), so 11 precedes 16.  And when 11 was placed on the second pile, the top card on the first pile was 7, so 7 is first in the subsequence.  Thus, </Font><Equation executable="false" input-equation="" display=""></Equation><Font style="Text"> is a strictly increasing subsequence of maximal length.</Font></Text-field>
<Text-field selection-placeholder="false" executable="false" family="Times New Roman" opaque="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" superscript="false" placeholder="false" readonly="false" subscript="false" bold="false" italic="false" underline="false" background="[255,255,255]" size="12" linebreak="space" spaceabove="10" rightmargin="0" bullet="none" firstindent="0" bulletsuffix="" linespacing="0.0" pagebreak-before="false" leftmargin="0" alignment="left" initial="0" spacebelow="0"><Font style="Text">You are encouraged to &quot;play&quot; through this approach a few times with your own sequences to ensure that you understand the process before continuing on to the implementation of the procedure below.  You can apply the </Font><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">FindIncreasing</Font><Font style="Text"> procedure defined below to make sure that you are arriving at the same result.  Be sure to keep track of what was on top of the previous pile when each number is played, as you need that information in the backtracking stage.</Font></Text-field>
<Text-field selection-placeholder="false" executable="false" family="Times New Roman" opaque="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" superscript="false" placeholder="false" readonly="false" subscript="false" bold="false" italic="false" underline="false" background="[255,255,255]" size="12" linebreak="space" spaceabove="10" rightmargin="0" bullet="none" firstindent="0" bulletsuffix="" linespacing="0.0" pagebreak-before="false" leftmargin="0" alignment="left" initial="0" spacebelow="0"><Font selection-placeholder="false" executable="false" family="Times New Roman" opaque="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" superscript="false" placeholder="false" readonly="false" subscript="false" bold="false" italic="true" underline="false" background="[255,255,255]" size="12">Implementing the algorithm</Font></Text-field><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L399" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Now we will implement the Patience algorithm.  The given sequence will be input to the procedure as a list.  Within the procedure, we need to track three kinds of information.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L397" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">First, we need to know which &quot;step&quot; we're in, that is, which card is being played.  This will be represented by the variable to a for loop ranging from 1 to the size of the list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L400" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Second, we need to know what cards are on the piles.  Specifically, we need to know the top card of each pile.  This will be represented as a list, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font>.  When a card is placed on top of an existing pile, we can replace the current value in that position with the the selection-assignment syntax <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles[i] := x</Font>;.  When a new pile is added to the list, we extend the list via the usual syntax <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">[op(piles),x];</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L401" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Third, in order to backtrack and recover the longest increasing sequence, we need to store, for each member of the sequence, the value that was on the top of the pile to the left of the entry's pile.  For this, we will use a table, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">pointers</Font>, whose indices will be the members of the sequence and whose entries will be set to the previous pile's top card.  (We use the name <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">pointers</Font> for this table because of the similarity to the &quot;linked list&quot; structure used in some programming languages.)  For those numbers played in the first pile, the value in the table will be set to <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">NULL</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L402" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The algorithm will consist of two stages.  The first stage will be the game stage.  After initializing <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font> to the empty list and <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">pointers</Font> to the empty table, we begin a for loop with loop variable <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">step</Font> running from 1 to the length of the input sequence <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">S</Font>.  Within this main loop, two tasks are performed.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L406" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The first thing that happens within the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">step</Font> loop is determining on which pile to play the current card.  Note that the value of the current card is accessed by <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">S[step]</Font>.  To determine the proper location for the current card, we do the following.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L408" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" bullet="numeric" bulletsuffix="." initial="1">Initialize a variable <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font> to 0.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L403" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" bullet="numeric" bulletsuffix="." initial="-1">Use a for loop from 1 to the current number of piles.  Within the for loop, compare the current card to the top of each pile.  If the current card is smaller than the value in <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font>, set <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font> equal to that pile index.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L409" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" bullet="numeric" bulletsuffix="." initial="-1">Once the loop exits, check the value of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font>.  If it is 0 following termination of the loop, that means a pile was not found for the current card, and thus the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font> list must be extended to create a new pile for this card.  Otherwise, the value of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font> is not 0 and we update the corresponding entry in <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font> to indicate that the latest card is placed on top in that position.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L412" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10" initial="-1">The second task within the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">step</Font> loop is to update the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">pointers</Font> table.  This also depends on the value of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L414" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" bullet="dot" initial="-1">If <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font> is 1 or if <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font> only contains 1 entry, then the latest card was played on the first pile and the associated value should be <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">NULL</Font>. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L416" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" bullet="dot" initial="-1">If <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font> is 0, then the value associated with the latest card is <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles[-2]</Font>, the top card on what had been the last pile but is now the next to last pile.  (Note that the previous condition, that <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">whichpile</Font> is 1 or <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font> has only 1 entry will ensure that this second condition is only tested if <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles</Font> has at least 2 entries and thus <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles[-2]</Font> is a valid selection.)  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L410" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" bullet="dot" initial="-1">Otherwise, the value is <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles[whichpile-1]</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L419" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">That concludes the game stage.  The second stage is the backtracking stage, which is much simpler.  First, access the top card of the last pile with <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">piles[-1]</Font>, and initialize the maximal increasing list, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">iList</Font>, to the list consisting of this value.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L417" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Then we extend <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">iList</Font> on the left with the entry in the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">pointers</Font> table associated to that value.  Since we're building the list from right to left, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">iList[1]</Font> always contains the most recently added number.  So <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">pointers[iList[1]]</Font> is the new value, which is added via the expression <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">[pointers[iList[1]],op(iList)]</Font>.  Since the cards played in the first pile were associated with <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">NULL</Font>, we can use a while loop with condition <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">pointers[iList[1]] &lt;&gt; NULL</Font> to fill the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">iList</Font>.  At the conclusion of the loop, the procedure returns <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">iList</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L420" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here, finally, is the procedure.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L421" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">FindIncreasing := proc(S::list)
  local piles, pointers, step, whichpile, p, iList;
  piles := [];
  pointers := table();
  # game stage
  for step from 1 to nops(S) do
    # playing the card
    whichpile := 0;
    for p from 1 to nops(piles) do
      if S[step] &lt; piles[p] then
        whichpile := p;
        break;
      end if;
    end do;
    if whichpile = 0 then
      piles := [op(piles),S[step]];
    else
      piles[whichpile] := S[step];
    end if;
    # update pointers
    if whichpile = 1 or nops(piles) = 1 then
      pointers[S[step]] := NULL;
    elif whichpile = 0 then
      pointers[S[step]] := piles[-2];
    else
      pointers[S[step]] := piles[whichpile-1];
    end if;
  end do;
  # backtracking stage
  iList := [piles[-1]];
  while pointers[iList[1]] &lt;&gt; NULL do
    iList := [pointers[iList[1]],op(iList)];
  end do;
  return iList;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L423" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Example 12 from the text involved the sequence <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L424" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">FindIncreasing([8,11,9,1,4,6,12,10,5,7]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyYiIiIiIiUiIiYiIig=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L426" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This is one of the four sequences given in the text.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L430" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Connection to the pigeonhole principle</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L431" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Recall that Theorem 3 asserted that every sequence of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> distinct real numbers contains a subsequence of length <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> that is either strictly increasing or strictly decreasing.  It may appear that the Patience algorithm has no connection to this theorem or to the pigeonhole principle.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L435" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">However, the Patience algorithm does in fact suggest a proof of Theorem 3 via the pigeonhole principle.  When the Patience algorithm is executed on a list of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> distinct real numbers, either there are at least <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> stacks or there are at most <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> stacks.  If there are <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> stacks, then there is a strictly increasing subsequence of length <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L436" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">On the other hand, assume that there are at most <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> stacks.  Take the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> values to be the pigeons and the stacks the pigeonholes.  When <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> objects are placed in <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> boxes, then there is a box containing at least <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> objects (Theorem 2).  Hence some stack has <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> values.  But the rules of the game ensure that each stack is a strictly decreasing subsequence, since one value is placed on top of another in a stack only when the second value is lesser than, and appears in the sequence later than, the lower value.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L437" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">In short, either there are <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> stacks and hence an increasing sequence of length <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> or there is a stack of size <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and hence a decreasing sequence of length <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">6.3 Permutations and Combinations</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L455" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">combinat</Font></Hyperlink> package contains many functions pertaining to counting and generating combinatorial structures.  We will be using <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">combinat</Font></Hyperlink> commands extensively in this section.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L458" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Permutations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L461" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We begin by looking at commands related to permutations of objects.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L459" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We have seen in previous chapters the use of the exclamation mark for factorial.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L462" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">6!;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiQ/KA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L464" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:factorial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">factorial</Font></Hyperlink> command can be used instead of the exclamation mark if you prefer.  Otherwise they are equivalent.  (Factorial does not depend on the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">combinat</Font></Hyperlink> package.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L486" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Counting permutations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L465" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">To compute the number of permutations, Maple provides the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> command.  This command can be used in a few different ways.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L468" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You can give only one argument, an integer.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L469" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbperm(5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiQ/Ig==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L472" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this case, Maple computes the number of permutations, that is, the number of 5-permutations of a set with 5 elements.  This, of course, is the same as computing <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L473" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Instead of an integer as the only argument, you can instead provide a set or list of objects.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L474" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbperm({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;});</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiQ/Ig==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L476" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">With this argument, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> computes the number of permutations of the given set (or list).  Since the set had five elements, this result is the same as the previous value.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L477" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To compute the number of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations of a set with <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects, use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> with a second argument indicating the value of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  The number of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNEYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNEYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation>-permutations of a set with <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiN0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiN0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation> distinct objects, that is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, is computed by the following command.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L478" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbperm(7,4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiRTKQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L480" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Once again, the first argument could be given as a set of a list of objects instead of the number of objects.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L481" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbperm([&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;,&quot;f&quot;,&quot;g&quot;],4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiRTKQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L483" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You may wonder what the purpose is of allowing the first argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> to be a set or list rather than requiring it to always be a positive integer.  We will explore this more in Section 6.5, but briefly, the reason is that the objects in a list do not have to be different.  By giving the first element as a list of not-necessarily distinct objects, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> will compute the number of arrangements of those objects, with repetition.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L487" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Listing permutations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L488" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">To obtain a list of all permutations, Maple provides the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> command.  The syntax is the same as <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink>, but instead of reporting the number of permutations, a list of the permutations is provided.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L489" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Once again, the first argument can be an integer or a set or a list.  If the first argument is given as an integer <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, Maple will display the permutations of the set <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  For example, the following command lists the permutations of the first four integers.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L490" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">permute(4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output"></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L492" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Providing a specific list or set as the first argument will produce the permutations of the objects in the given set or list.  For example, the permutations of the letters a, b, and c are shown below.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L493" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">permute({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;});</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">Nyg3JVEiYTYiUSJiRiVRImNGJTclRiRGJ0YmNyVGJkYkRic3JUYmRidGJDclRidGJEYmNyVGJ0YmRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L495" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To obtain the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations instead, you must provide <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> as the second argument.  The following lists the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiMkYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiMkYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation>-permutations of a set with 4 distinct objects.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L496" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">permute(4,2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">Ny43JCIiIiIiIzckRiQiIiQ3JEYkIiIlNyRGJUYkNyRGJUYnNyRGJUYpNyRGJ0YkNyRGJ0YlNyRGJ0YpNyRGKUYkNyRGKUYlNyRGKUYn</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L498" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Or you can provide your own objects to be <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permuted.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L499" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">permute({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;},2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NzY3JFEiYTYiUSJiRiU3JEYkUSJjRiU3JEYkUSJkRiU3JEYkUSJlRiU3JEYmRiQ3JEYmRig3JEYmRio3JEYmRiw3JEYoRiQ3JEYoRiY3JEYoRio3JEYoRiw3JEYqRiQ3JEYqRiY3JEYqRig3JEYqRiw3JEYsRiQ3JEYsRiY3JEYsRig3JEYsRio=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L501" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Random permutations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L502" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple also provides a command, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink>, that will produce a randomly chosen permutation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L503" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Once again, the argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink> can be an integer or a list or set of objects.  If an integer <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is given, then <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink> returns a randomly selected permutation of the set<Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L504" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randperm(10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NywiIzUiIiIiIigiIikiIiUiIiYiIiMiIiciIiQiIio=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L506" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">If a list or set is provided as the first argument, then the random permutation is produced using the elements of the given set or list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L507" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randperm([&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyZRImM2IlEiYkYkUSJkRiRRImFGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L509" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that the permutation is selected so that each permutation has the same probability of being chosen.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L510" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Unlike the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> commands, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink> does not accept a second argument.  (If one is given, it is ignored.)  In order to produce a random <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutation, you must use one of the following approaches.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L511" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Suppose you need a random <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNEYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNEYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation>-permutation of the set <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Your first choice is to apply the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink> command to the set, and then use the selection operator to select the first four elements.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L512" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randperm({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;,&quot;f&quot;})[1..4];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyZRImU2IlEiYkYkUSJmRiRRImNGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L514" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The second option is to first use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randcomb</Font></Hyperlink> (described below) to obtain a random subset of size 4 and then apply <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink> to the randomly chosen subset.  This approach will be demonstrated below, after describing the commands relevant to combinations.  Both approaches will produce a random <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutation of the given objects.  The second approach is faster to execute, however.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L515" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Combinations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L516" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The commands related to combinations are very similar to those for permutations.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L517" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Counting combinations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L518" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The number of combinations is obtained with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink> command.  Like <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink>, this command accepts one or two arguments and the first argument can be a number or a set or list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L519" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">If you provide only one argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink>, it returns the total number of combinations. For instance,</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L520" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcomb(5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNL</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L522" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">indicates that there are 32 ways to choose some (including all or none) of five objects.  In other words, there are 32 subsets of a set of 5 elements.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L523" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The first argument can also be a set or a list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L524" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcomb({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;});</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNL</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L526" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To compute the number of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-combinations, you provide <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> as the second argument.  Once again, the first argument can be a set or list or a positive integer indicating the size of the set the objects are to be drawn from.  The following compute <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and the number of ways to choose 3 from the set of vowels.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L527" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcomb(52,5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IihnKilmIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L528" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcomb({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;e&quot;,&quot;i&quot;,&quot;o&quot;,&quot;u&quot;},3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM1</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L532" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The text mentions that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is also referred to as a binomial coefficient.  In Maple, the command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> can also be used to compute <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  For example, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> can be computed by:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L534" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">binomial(52,5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IihnKilmIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L536" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink> agree when given two nonnegative integers as arguments.  However, they are not identical commands.  For one, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> requires two arguments which must evaluate to algebraic expressions and cannot accept a list or set as the first argument.  On the other hand, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> uses a more general formula that can compute with rational and floating-point arguments.  Also, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> is not part of the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">combinat</Font></Hyperlink> package.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L530" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Listing Combinations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L531" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink> command is the combination analog of <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L537" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Given only one argument, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink> produces all possible combinations of every size.  If the argument is a set or list, it will list the subsets or sublists of the argument.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L538" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">choose({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;});</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PDI8IjwjUSJhNiI8I1EiYkYmPCNRImNGJjwjUSJkRiY8JEYlRig8JEYlRio8JEYlRiw8JEYoRio8JEYoRiw8JEYqRiw8JUYlRihGKjwlRiVGKEYsPCVGJUYqRiw8JUYoRipGLDwmRiVGKEYqRiw=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L539" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">choose([&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NzI3IjcjUSJhNiI3I1EiYkYmNyNRImNGJjcjUSJkRiY3JEYlRig3JEYlRio3JEYlRiw3JEYoRio3JEYoRiw3JEYqRiw3JUYlRihGKjclRiVGKEYsNyVGJUYqRiw3JUYoRipGLDcmRiVGKEYqRiw=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L541" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that if the argument is a set, the result is a set of sets.  On the other hand, if the argument is a list, the result is a list of lists.  Many Maple commands accept either sets or lists as arguments, and they typically return the same kind of object they were given.  This was not the case for <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink>, because in that case order is relevant, so it must return lists.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L542" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">If the argument is a non-negative integer <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, it will use <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> by default.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L543" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">choose(3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">Nyo3IjcjIiIiNyMiIiM3IyIiJDckRiVGJzckRiVGKTckRidGKTclRiVGJ0Yp</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L550" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that with only one argument, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink> behaves very similarly to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,powerset" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">powerset</Font></Hyperlink>, which was discussed in Section 2.1 of this manual.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,powerset" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">powerset</Font></Hyperlink> command accepts only one argument, which can be a set, list, or non-negative integer.  It returns the set of all subsets (or the list of all sublists) just as <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink> does.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L551" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">powerset({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;});</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCo8IjwjUSJhNiI8I1EiYkYmPCNRImNGJjwkRiVGKDwkRiVGKjwkRihGKjwlRiVGKEYq</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L545" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">With a second argument given to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink>, you can specify the number of objects to be selected.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L546" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">choose({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;},2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCg8JFEiYTYiUSJiRiU8JEYkUSJjRiU8JEYkUSJkRiU8JEYmRig8JEYmRio8JEYoRio=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L547" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">choose(5,2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">Nyw3JCIiIiIiIzckRiQiIiQ3JEYkIiIlNyRGJCIiJjckRiVGJzckRiVGKTckRiVGKzckRidGKTckRidGKzckRilGKw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L549" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,powerset" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">powerset</Font></Hyperlink> command does not allow a second argument.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L554" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Random combinations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L558" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randcomb</Font></Hyperlink> command is used to produce a random combination.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L555" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As opposed to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randcomb</Font></Hyperlink> requires two arguments.  The first can be a set or list of objects or a positive integer, and the second must be a non-negative integer.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L559" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randcomb({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;},3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCVRImE2IlEiY0YkUSJkRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L560" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randcomb(5,3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCUiIiIiIiQiIiU=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L562" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As mentioned earlier, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randcomb</Font></Hyperlink> can be combined with <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink> to produce a random permutation of a specified size.  To obtain a random 4-permutation of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, for example, first use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randcomb</Font></Hyperlink> to pick a random 4-combination.  Then apply <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,randperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">randperm</Font></Hyperlink> to the selected combination.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L564" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randcomb({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;,&quot;f&quot;},4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCZRImE2IlEiYkYkUSJlRiRRImZGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L565" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randperm(%);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyZRImY2IlEiZUYkUSJiRiRRImFGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L567" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You can combine them into one statement, as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L568" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randperm(randcomb({&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;,&quot;d&quot;,&quot;e&quot;,&quot;f&quot;},4));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyZRImE2IlEiZUYkUSJiRiRRImNGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L570" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Circular Permutations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L571" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The prelude to Exercises 40 and 41 of Section 6.3 describes circular permutations.  A circular <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutation of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> people is a seating of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> of those <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> people at a circular table.  Moreover, two seatings are considered the same if one can be obtained from the other by rotation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L572" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The exercises ask you to compute the number of circular <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiM0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiM0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation>-permutations of 5 people and to arrive at a formula for that number.  In this subsection, we'll write a procedure to list all of the circular <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> people.  Having such a procedure can help you more easily explore the concept and test your formula.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1297" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L609" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Rotating a permutation</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L611" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The key to listing all circular permutations is to devise a way to test whether two circular permutations are equal.  According to the definition, two circular permutations are considered to be equal if one can be obtained from the other by a rotation.  If we use a list to represent a permutation, a rotation will consist of moving the first element to the end of the list (or the last to the front).</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L574" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here is a procedure that, given a list representing a circular permutation, will rotate the permutation by one position.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L575" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">RotatePerm := proc(P::list)
  return [op(2..-1,P),P[1]];
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L577" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, the seating Abe, Carol, Barbara, is the same as Carol, Barbara, Abe.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L578" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">RotatePerm([&quot;Abe&quot;,&quot;Carol&quot;,&quot;Barbara&quot;]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyVRJkNhcm9sNiJRKEJhcmJhcmFGJFEkQWJlRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L580" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that rotations start to repeat.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L581" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">RotatePerm(%);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyVRKEJhcmJhcmE2IlEkQWJlRiRRJkNhcm9sRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L582" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">RotatePerm(%);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyVRJEFiZTYiUSZDYXJvbEYkUShCYXJiYXJhRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L584" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In fact, for an <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutation, after <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> rotations, the list will return to its original state.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L613" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Equality of circular permutations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L585" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This observation indicates that, given two <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations, we can test to see if they are the same by rotating one of them <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> times.  The procedure below returns true if the two input lists represent the same circular permutation and false otherwise.  It first checks equality without performing rotation.  Then, using a for loop, it rotates the second list, checking for equality after each rotation.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L586" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">CPEquals := proc(L1::list,L2::list)
  local i, Lr;
  if L1 = L2 then 
    return true;
  end if;
  Lr := L2;
  for i from 1 to nops(L2)-1 do
    Lr := RotatePerm(Lr);
    if L1 = Lr then
      return true;
    end if;
  end do;
  return false;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L588" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We can use it to confirm equality of circular permutations.  For example, </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L589" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">CPEquals([&quot;Charles&quot;,&quot;Helen&quot;,&quot;Dean&quot;],[&quot;Helen&quot;,&quot;Dean&quot;,&quot;Charles&quot;]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L615" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Listing all circular permutations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L591" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Now we are prepared to write a procedure that lists all circular permutations.  First, we'll use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> command to generate all <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> people.  We will initialize the set of all distinct circular permutations to the the first element of the list of all permutations.  That first permutation is then removed from the list of all permutations.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L597" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Within a while loop, consider the first element in the list of all permutations.  Use <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">CPEquals</Font> and a loop to see if the first element is identical to any of the members of the set of circular permutations.  If not, add it to the set of circular permutations.  In either case, it is deleted from the list of all permutations.  This continues until the list of all permutations has been emptied.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L599" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here is the implementation.  Our procedure will accept a set as the first argument and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, the number that can be seated at the table, as the second argument.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L600" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">AllCP := proc(S::set,r::posint)
  local allP, allCP, isnew, p;
  allP := combinat[permute](S,r);
  allCP := {allP[1]};
  allP := allP[2..-1];
  while allP &lt;&gt; [] do
    isnew := true;
    for p in allCP do
      if CPEquals(allP[1],p) then
        isnew := false;
        break;
      end if;
    end do;
    if isnew then
      allCP := allCP union {allP[1]};
    end if;
    allP := allP[2..-1];
  end do;
  return allCP;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L602" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">isnew</Font> boolean is used to track whether or not the current first member of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">allP</Font> is new or not.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L603" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The following computes the possible circular 3-permutations of the set {&quot;Abe&quot;, &quot;Barbara&quot;, &quot;Carol&quot;, &quot;Dean&quot;, &quot;Eve&quot;}.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L604" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">AllCP({&quot;Abe&quot;,&quot;Barbara&quot;,&quot;Carol&quot;,&quot;Dean&quot;,&quot;Eve&quot;},3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PDY3JVEkQWJlNiJRKEJhcmJhcmFGJVEmQ2Fyb2xGJTclRiRGJlElRGVhbkYlNyVGJEYmUSRFdmVGJTclRiRGJ0YmNyVGJEYnRik3JUYkRidGKzclRiRGKUYmNyVGJEYpRic3JUYkRilGKzclRiRGK0YmNyVGJEYrRic3JUYkRitGKTclRiZGJ0YpNyVGJkYnRis3JUYmRilGJzclRiZGKUYrNyVGJkYrRic3JUYmRitGKTclRidGKUYrNyVGJ0YrRik=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L605" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">nops(%);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM/</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L607" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">It is left to the reader to experiment with other starting sets and values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> to determine a formula.  Note that the procedures in this subsection were written using a very naive approach.  There are simpler and more efficient approaches, but those would give away the key idea used to create the formula.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">6.4 Binomial Coefficients and Identities</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L617" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this section we will use Maple to compute binomial coefficients, to generate Pascal's Triangle, and to verify identities.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L618" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">The Binomial Theorem</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L619" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Recall from the previous section that the Maple command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> can be used to compute <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, which is another notation for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  With <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> positive integers, this command produces the same result as <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink>.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> command is designed to be more general, in that it will compute coefficients that appear in Newton's generalized binomial theorem.  The generalization is beyond the scope of this manual.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1284" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here, we will consider questions such as Examples 2 through 4 from section 6.4 of the text.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L623" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">First consider the problem of expanding <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  In Maple, this can be done easily with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:expand" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">expand</Font></Hyperlink> command.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:expand" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">expand</Font></Hyperlink> command requires one argument, an algebraic expressions.  It returns the result of &quot;expanding&quot; the expression, that is, of distributing products over sums.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L624" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">expand((x+y)^5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LC4qJClJInhHNiIiIiYiIiJGKCooRidGKClGJSIiJUYoSSJ5R0YmRihGKCooIiM1RigpRiUiIiRGKClGLCIiI0YoRigqKEYuRigpRiVGMkYoKUYsRjBGKEYoKihGJ0YoRiVGKClGLEYrRihGKCokKUYsRidGKEYo</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L621" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Now consider the question of finding the coefficient of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> in the expansion of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  The binomial theorem tells us that this coefficient is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> command with first argument 30 and second 12 will produce this value.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L626" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">binomial(30,12);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IilES1wnKQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L628" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Thus, the expansion of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> contains the term <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L629" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Finding the coefficient of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> in the expansion of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> requires that we include the coefficients of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> in the computation.  As explained in the solution to Example 4 of the text, the expansion is</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L630" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L632" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The coefficient of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is found by taking <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L633" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L634" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This is</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L635" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">binomial(25,13)*2^12*(-3)^13;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">ITIrQ3FYTndmUiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L637" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Pascal's Triangle</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L638" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">As we've seen, it is very easy to compute binomial coefficients with Maple.  To compute row <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> of Pascal's triangle, we apply the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:binomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">binomial</Font></Hyperlink> command with the second argument ranging from 0 to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>. This can be done with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:seq" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">seq</Font></Hyperlink> command.  For example, the 25th row of Pascal's triangle is shown below.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L639" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">seq(binomial(25,k),k=0..25);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NjwiIiIiI0QiJCskIiUrQiImXUUiIiZJSiYiJytyPCInKzJbIih2OjMiIih2SC8jIihnKG9LIigrdVglIigrLj8mRi9GLkYtRixGK0YqRilGKEYnRiZGJUYkRiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L643" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">When calculating a single binomial coefficient or an isolated row, applying the formula may be the most efficient approach.  But if you wish to build a sizable portion of Pascal's Triangle, making use of Pascal's Identity (Theorem 2 of Section 6.4) and the symmetry property (Corollary 2 of Section 6.3) can be more effective.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L641" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">In this subsection, we'll write two procedures for computing binomial coefficients.  The first will simply apply the formula <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  The second will be a recursive procedure making use of Pascal's Identity and symmetry.  Then we'll compare the performance of the two procedures in building Pascal's Triangle.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L645" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The first procedure will be a straightforward application of the formula.  We name it <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BinomialF</Font> (for formula).</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L646" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">BinomialF := proc(n::nonnegint, k::nonnegint)
  return n!/(k!*(n-k)!);
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L656" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">A recursive procedure</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L648" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The second procedure will be called <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BinomialR</Font> (for recursive).  Recall Pascal's Identity:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L649" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L650" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Rewriting this in terms of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, we have</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L651" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L652" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Also recall that the binomial coefficients are symmetric, that is,</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L653" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L654" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">With these facts in mind, our recursive procedure will work as follows.  We will declare the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:remember" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">remember</Font></Hyperlink> option so that Maple will automatically create a remember table.  The body of the procedure will consist of an if-elif-else statement.  In case the second argument, is 0, the procedure will return 1.  That forms the basis case of the recursion.  The elif clause will test whether <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  In this case, we make use of symmetry and call <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BinomialR</Font> on <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Finally, in the else clause, we apply Pascal's Identity, making recursive calls to <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BinomialR</Font>.  Here is the implementation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L657" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">BinomialR := proc(n::nonnegint, k::nonnegint)
  option remember;
  if k=0 then
    return 1;
  elif 2*k &gt; n then
    return BinomialR(n,n-k);
  else
    return BinomialR(n-1,k-1) + BinomialR(n-1,k);
  end if;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L659" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We use our two procedures to build Pascal's triangle using a loop to control the value of n and the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:seq" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">seq</Font></Hyperlink> command.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L660" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">for n from 0 to 5 do
  seq(BinomialF(n,k),k=0..n);
end do;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiQiIiJGIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiUiIiIiIiNGIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiYiIiIiIiRGJEYj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiciIiIiIiUiIidGJEYj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NigiIiIiIiYiIzVGJUYkRiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L661" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">for n from 0 to 5 do
  seq(BinomialR(n,k),k=0..n);
end do;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiQiIiJGIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiUiIiIiIiNGIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiYiIiIiIiRGJEYj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiciIiIiIiUiIidGJEYj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NigiIiIiIiYiIzVGJUYkRiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L663" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Comparing performance</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L671" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Now we'll compare the performance of the two procedures.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L664" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">First, we'll use each of them to compute the first 1000 rows of Pascal's Triangle and compare the time it takes.  We must use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:forget" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">forget</Font></Hyperlink> to clear the remember table for <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BinomialR</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L665" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">forget(BinomialR);</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L666" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">st := time():
for n from 0 to 1000 do
  seq(BinomialF(n,k),k=0..n):
end do:
time() - st;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">JCImLTAjISIk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L667" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">st := time():
for n from 0 to 1000 do
  seq(BinomialR(n,k),k=0..n):
end do:
time() - st;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">JCIlYEEhIiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L669" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">You see that the difference between the two performance of the two procedures is substantial.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L675" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">However, if we compute some isolated values, the situation is reversed.  We will generate some random values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> between 100 and 1000 and random values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> between 0 and 100.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L672" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Recall that the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:rand" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">rand</Font></Hyperlink> command applied to a range of integers returns a procedure that generates random numbers in that range.  For instance, the statement below assigns to the name <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">randN</Font> a procedure.  Calling <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">randN()</Font> produces a random number.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L676" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randN := rand(100..1000):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L677" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randN();</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiQnSA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L679" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Likewise for <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">randK</Font>.  Note that calling <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:rand" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">rand</Font></Hyperlink> with a single integer as the argument is the same as the range from 0 to that value.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L680" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randK := rand(100):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L681" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">randK();</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNF</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L683" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Now we'll generate a list of 100 randomly generated <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> pairs.  We will suppress the output since the list will be long.  But note that the elements of this list are 2-element lists.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L684" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">NKlist := [seq([randN(),randK()],i=1..100)]:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L686" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Now we apply the two procedures to the elements of the list.  Note that we pass the arguments to both procedures with the syntax <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BinomialF(op(NKpair))</Font>.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:op" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">op</Font></Hyperlink> command applied to <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">NKpair</Font>, an element of the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">NKlist</Font>, takes the 2-element list <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">NKpair</Font> and returns the underlying expression sequence.  The procedures were written to accept two integer arguments, so passing them the 2-element list (which is only one argument) would raise an error.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L689" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">st := time():
for NKpair in NKlist do
  BinomialF(op(NKpair)):
end do:
time() - st;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">JCIiJSEiJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L690" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">forget(BinomialR);</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L691" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">st := time():
for NKpair in NKlist do
  BinomialR(op(NKpair)):
end do:
time() - st;
  </Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">JCIkdyMhIiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L693" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The reason for the difference in relative performance is that when generating Pascal's Triangle, all of the values beginning with <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> needed to be calculated.  On the other hand when calculating certain isolated values, the recursive procedure still had to calculate all of the results for lower values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, which the non-recursive procedure did not need to compute.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L694" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Verifying Identities</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L697" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple can help verify identities regarding the binomial coefficients.  If you enter an expression using the binomial command that includes symbolic arguments, Maple will echo the statement.  First, we ensure that <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">n</Font> and <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">k</Font> are unassigned.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L698" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">n := 'n'; k := 'k';</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">SSJuRzYi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">SSJrRzYi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L699" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">binomial(n,k);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">LUkpYmlub21pYWxHNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHNiI2JEkibkdGJ0kia0dGJw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L701" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To have Maple produce an expression using factorials, use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,factorial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">convert</Font></Hyperlink> command with the binomial expression as the first argument and the keyword <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">factorial</Font> as the second argument.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L702" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(binomial(n,k),factorial);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KigtSSpmYWN0b3JpYWxHJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHNiNJIm5HNiIiIiItRiQ2I0kia0dGKCEiIi1GJDYjLCZGJ0YpRixGLUYt</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L705" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Verifying symmetry</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L706" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">As a first example, we'll verify the identity <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, the symmetry identity.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L707" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Assign names to the left and right hand sides of the identity.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L708" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">left := binomial(n,k);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUkpYmlub21pYWxHNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHNiI2JEkibkdGJ0kia0dGJw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L709" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">right := binomial(n,n-k);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUkpYmlub21pYWxHNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHNiI2JEkibkdGJywmRikiIiJJImtHRichIiI=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L711" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Remember that we need to use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:evalb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">evalb</Font></Hyperlink> command to evaluate boolean expressions, such as the equality <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">left=right</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L712" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(left=right);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L714" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The negative result illustrates that a great deal of care is needed when using Maple to verify identities.  Maple does not recognize the above as a true statement and therefore reports false.  That does not mean that the identity is not in fact true.  It only indicates that Maple was not able to confirm it.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L715" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">In order to verify the symmetry identity, we need to apply two other commands.  First, we'll use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">convert</Font></Hyperlink> command to replace the definitions of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">left</Font> and <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">right</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L716" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">left := convert(left,factorial);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KigtSSpmYWN0b3JpYWxHJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHNiNJIm5HNiIiIiItRiQ2I0kia0dGKCEiIi1GJDYjLCZGJ0YpRixGLUYt</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L717" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">right := convert(right,factorial);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KigtSSpmYWN0b3JpYWxHJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHNiNJIm5HNiIiIiItRiQ2I0kia0dGKCEiIi1GJDYjLCZGJ0YpRixGLUYt</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L719" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Second, we'll use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink> to help ensure that Maple will perform necessary algebraic manipulations in order for it to recognize the equality.  It is often simplest to embed the call to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink> within the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:evalb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">evalb</Font></Hyperlink> statement.  (In this example, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink> is not necessary, but it is typically needed.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L720" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(simplify(left=right));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L722" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">The hockeystick identity</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L723" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Exercise 27 in Section 6.4 asks you to prove the hockeystick identity:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L724" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L725" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The exercise asks you to prove it using a combinatorial argument and using Pascal's identity.  Maple will verify this identity for us using algebra based on the formula for the binomial coefficient.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L726" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">First, we will give the right hand side of the identity a name.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L727" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">rightHockey := binomial(n+r+1,r);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUkpYmlub21pYWxHNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHNiI2JCwoSSJuR0YnIiIiSSJyR0YnRitGK0YrRiw=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L729" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The left hand side is a summation.  Since it is a symbolic sum, we must use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:sum" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">sum</Font></Hyperlink>, rather than <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> to represent it.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L730" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">leftHockey := sum(binomial(n+k,k),k=0..r);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KigsJkkickc2IiIiIkYmRiZGJiwmSSJuR0YlRiZGJkYmISIiLUkpYmlub21pYWxHNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHRiU2JCwoRihGJkYkRiZGJkYmRiNGJg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L732" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Notice that Maple was able to use information it knows about binomial coefficients in order to do some simplification of the summation.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L737" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To verify the identity, we need to go a step further and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">convert</Font></Hyperlink> the expressions to factorials.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L738" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">leftHockey := convert(leftHockey,factorial);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KiwsJkkickc2IiIiIkYmRiZGJiwmSSJuR0YlRiZGJkYmISIiLUkqZmFjdG9yaWFsRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRzYjLChGKEYmRiRGJkYmRiZGJi1GKzYjRiNGKS1GKzYjRihGKQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L739" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">rightHockey := convert(rightHockey,factorial);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KigtSSpmYWN0b3JpYWxHJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHNiMsKEkibkc2IiIiIkkickdGKUYqRipGKkYqLUYkNiNGKyEiIi1GJDYjLCZGKEYqRipGKkYu</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L741" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Now <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:evalb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">evalb</Font></Hyperlink> combined with <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink> will confirm the identity.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L742" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(simplify(leftHockey=rightHockey));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L744" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Keep in mind when using Maple to check identities that it will only report true when the two sides of the expression are identical.  If it does report true, you can be fairly confident that the identity does hold, though a truly convincing proof requires that you explicitly show the algebraic manipulations.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L745" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">If Maple reports false, however, even after using <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">convert</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink>, you can not be certain whether the identity is false or if it is true but more manipulation is needed to get Maple to recognize it.  To use Maple to demonstrate that a purported identity is false, you would find a counterexample by computing the values of both expressions and finding inputs that result in different values.  (Refer to Section 1.7 for examples of finding counterexamples.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">6.5 Generalized Permutations and Combinations</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L747" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this section we will introduce a variety of Maple commands related to permutations and combinations with repetition allowed and related to distributing objects in boxes where the objects and the boxes may or may not be distinguishable.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L748" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Permutations with Repetition</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L749" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Recall from Theorem 1 that the number of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1cEdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRInJGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUxc3VwZXJzY3JpcHRzaGlmdEdRIjBGJy9GNlEnbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1cEdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRInJGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUxc3VwZXJzY3JpcHRzaGlmdEdRIjBGJy9GNlEnbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> if repetition is allowed.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L750" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, the number of strings of length 5 that can be formed from the 26 uppercase letters of the English alphabet is</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L751" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">26^5;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">Iil3OCk9Ig==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L753" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As a second example, we compute the number of ways that four elements can be selected in order from a set with three elements when repetition is allowed.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L770" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">3^4;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiMiKQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L772" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Recall from the previous section that the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> commands can accept either a number or a list or a set as the first argument.  In case you provide a list as the first argument, that list may have repeated elements.  In case the list does contain repeated elements, those are allowed to repeat in the permutations.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L773" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, consider the statement below.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L774" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">permute([1,1,2]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyU3JSIiIkYkIiIjNyVGJEYlRiQ3JUYlRiRGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L776" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Given a list of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> not necessarily distinct objects and no second argument, the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> command produces all of the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations of the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects.  Note that 1 appeared twice in the input and thus appears twice in the results, while 2 appeared once in the input and so appears once in the output.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L777" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">With a second argument, you can specify the length of the permutations.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L778" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">permute([1,1,2],2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyU3JCIiIkYkNyRGJCIiIzckRiZGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L780" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Since 1 appeared twice in the input list, it was allowed to appear twice in the results, but 2 appeared only one time in the input and thus was not allowed to repeat.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L781" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">This means that if you want to list the ways that four elements can be selected in order from a set with three elements when repetition is allowed, you can use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> command, so long as you repeat the elements in the input.  In order to generate all <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects with repetition allowed, you must use as input the list consisting of the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects each repeated <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> times.  If an object is repeated fewer than <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> times in the input list, then that will limit the number of times it is allowed to repeat in the results.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L782" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To form 4-permutations with repetition allowed of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, we apply the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> command as shown below.  Recall the use of the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:dollar" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">dollar sign operator</Font></Hyperlink> with syntax <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">o $ n</Font> to produce a sequence of the object <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">o</Font> repeated <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">n</Font> times.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L783" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">abcRepeated := permute([&quot;a&quot; $ 4, &quot;b&quot; $ 4, &quot;c&quot; $ 4],4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L784" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">nops(abcRepeated);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiMiKQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L787" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that the size of the list produced by <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> agrees with the answer given by the formula <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1cEdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRInJGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUxc3VwZXJzY3JpcHRzaGlmdEdRIjBGJy9GNlEnbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1cEdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRInJGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUxc3VwZXJzY3JpcHRzaGlmdEdRIjBGJy9GNlEnbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L788" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> command has the same syntax as the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> command and will return the number of permutations without listing them all.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L789" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbperm([&quot;a&quot; $ 4, &quot;b&quot; $ 4, &quot;c&quot; $ 4],4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiMiKQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L756" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Combinations with Repetition</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L769" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Combinations with repetition can be handled in Maple in much the same way as permutations with repetition are.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L792" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Theorem 2 of Section 6.5, asserts that the number of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-combinations of a set of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects when repetition of elements is allowed is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  This suggests the following useful functional operator.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L793" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcombrep := (n,r) -&gt; numbcomb(n+r-1,r):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L797" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Thus we can compute, for example, the number of ways to select five bills from a cash box with seven types of bills (Example 3) as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L798" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcombrep(7,5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiRpJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L800" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We can also make use of the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink> commands in the same way as <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> were used above.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L801" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Consider Example 2 from Section 6.5.  In this example, we are given a bowl of apples, oranges, and pears and are to select four pieces of fruit from the bowl provided that it contains at least four pieces of each kind of fruit.  We have three ways to solve this problem with Maple.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L803" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">First, we can use the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">numbcombrep</Font> functional operator that we created.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L804" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcombrep(3,4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM6</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L806" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The second approach is to use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink>.  The first argument is the list of &quot;apple&quot;, &quot;orange&quot;, and &quot;pear&quot; each repeated four times, and the second argument is 4 indicating that four objects are to be selected.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L807" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcomb([&quot;apple&quot; $ 4, &quot;orange&quot; $ 4, &quot;pear&quot; $ 4],4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM6</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L809" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The third option is to use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink> to list all the options.  The arguments are the same as they were for <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L810" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">choose([&quot;apple&quot; $ 4, &quot;orange&quot; $ 4, &quot;pear&quot; $ 4],4);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NzE3JlEmYXBwbGU2IkYkRiRGJDcmRiRGJEYkUSdvcmFuZ2VGJTcmRiRGJEYkUSVwZWFyRiU3JkYkRiRGJ0YnNyZGJEYkRidGKTcmRiRGJEYpRik3JkYkRidGJ0YnNyZGJEYnRidGKTcmRiRGJ0YpRik3JkYkRilGKUYpNyZGJ0YnRidGJzcmRidGJ0YnRik3JkYnRidGKUYpNyZGJ0YpRilGKTcmRilGKUYpRik=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L811" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">nops(%);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM6</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L813" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that when repetition is allowed, all of the commands <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink>, and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,choose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">choose</Font></Hyperlink> must be given a list as the first argument.  If you were to use a set instead of a list, the repeated elements in the set would be automatically removed by Maple.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L815" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Permutations with Indistinguishable Objects</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L816" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple handles permutations with indistinguishable objects in the same way as when repetition is allowed.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> commands both accept lists of objects as their first argument.  If objects in the list are repeated, Maple considers them as indistinguishable and counts the permutations appropriately.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L817" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, to solve Example 7, finding the number of different strings that can be made from the letters of the word <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEoU1VDQ0VTU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEoU1VDQ0VTU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, we use the list <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> as the argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbperm" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbperm</Font></Hyperlink>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L818" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbperm([&quot;S&quot;,&quot;U&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;E&quot;,&quot;S&quot;,&quot;S&quot;]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiQ/JQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L820" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Observe that this gives the same result as the formula given in Theorem 3.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L821" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Maple makes it easy to go a bit further than Theorem 3, which is restricted to the situation when you are permuting all <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects.  To find the number of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> objects where some objects are indistinguishable, you give <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> as the second argument.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L822" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, the number of strings of length 3 that can be made from the letters of the word <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEoU1VDQ0VTU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEoU1VDQ0VTU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> can be calculated as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L823" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbperm([&quot;S&quot;,&quot;U&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;E&quot;,&quot;S&quot;,&quot;S&quot;],3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L825" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Listing these words can be accomplished by use of the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,permute" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">permute</Font></Hyperlink> command with the same arguments.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L827" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">permute([&quot;S&quot;,&quot;U&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;E&quot;,&quot;S&quot;,&quot;S&quot;],3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output"></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L831" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">A related question is the number of strings with 3 or more letters that can be made from the letters of the word <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEoU1VDQ0VTU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEoU1VDQ0VTU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  To do this, we compute the number of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-permutations with <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> equal to 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7 and sum these values.  Using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> command, this can be done in one statement.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L829" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Remember that the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> command accepts a first argument in terms of a variable such as <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">i</Font>.  With the second argument specifying the range of values that <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">i</Font> can take, the command returns the sum of the numbers obtained by evaluating the first argument at each value for <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">i</Font>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L833" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">add(numbperm([&quot;S&quot;,&quot;U&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;C&quot;,&quot;E&quot;,&quot;S&quot;,&quot;S&quot;],i),i=3..7);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiVaNw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L832" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Distinguishable Objects and Distinguishable Boxes</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L835" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Example 8 asks how many ways there are to distribute hands of 5 cards to each of four players from a deck of 52 cards.  There are several ways to compute this value in Maple.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L836" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">First, we can use the expression in terms of combinations, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> by using <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomb</Font></Hyperlink>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L837" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcomb(52,5)*numbcomb(47,5)*numbcomb(42,5)*numbcomb(37,5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IjpTVS4/U2t2TSVHRXk5</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L839" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Second, we can use the formula from Theorem 4: <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L840" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">52!/(5!*5!*5!*5!*32!);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IjpTVS4/U2t2TSVHRXk5</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L842" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that when multiplying factorials, the asterisk is required.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L843" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Finally, this same value can be computed using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,multinomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">multinomial</Font></Hyperlink> command.  This command requires at least two positive integers as arguments, but can accept any number of positive integers.  Regardless of the number of arguments, the first must be equal to the sum of the rest.  If <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is the first argument and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> are the rest of the arguments, then assuming <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, the command returns</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L844" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L845" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">That is, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,multinomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">multinomial</Font></Hyperlink> implements the formula of Theorem 4.  (Exercise 63 of Section 6.5 explains the reason for the term multinomial.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L846" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Computing the number of ways to deal cards, as described above, can be computed as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L847" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">multinomial(52,5,5,5,5,32);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IjpTVS4/U2t2TSVHRXk5</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L851" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Revising the multinomial command</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L849" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">It is common in questions about distributing distinguishable objects into distinguishable boxes that you want to distribute only some of the objects.  In Example 8, for instance, not all of the cards are distributed.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,multinomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">multinomial</Font></Hyperlink> command requires that you include the remainder of the cards as an argument.  Conceptually, you can think of making one more box to hold the objects that are not placed in any of the other boxes.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L852" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">This is such a common occurrence, however, that it seems more natural to forget about this &quot;discard box.&quot;  We will write a Maple command that will use the formula from Theorem 4 but will not require that we provide the size of the discard box.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L853" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">In order to make our procedure work as much like <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,multinomial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">multinomial</Font></Hyperlink> as possible, we need it to be able to accept any number of arguments.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L856" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To do this, we apply the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:parameter_modifiers" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">seq</Font><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]"> modifier</Font></Hyperlink> to the parameter.  As a simple example of how this works, consider the example below.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1215" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">printInts := proc(x::seq(integer))
  print(x);
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1217" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The parameter declaration <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">x::seq(integer)</Font> indicates that the parameter is to accept a sequence of integers.  When the procedure is called with a sequence of integers as arguments, the name <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">x</Font> is assigned to the sequence.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1218" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">printInts(5,2,9,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiYiIiYiIiMiIioiIzY=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1219" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">If non-integers are included in the call to the procedure, the parameter will only be assigned to the sequence of integers up until the first non-integer.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1220" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">printInts(-5,8,2,&quot;hello&quot;,7,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiUhIiYiIikiIiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1221" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The type in parentheses after the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">seq</Font> keyword can be any Maple type.  Also note that such a parameter can match the empty sequence in which case it is assigned <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">NULL</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L863" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Our procedure will have two parameters.  The first will be a positive integer <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">n</Font>.  The second will be the sequence of positive integers <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">k</Font>.  First, we ensure that <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">k</Font> is not empty.  (If <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">k = NULL</Font>, the procedure returns 1, as there is 1 way to discard all the objects.)  Then we calculate the difference of the parameter <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">n</Font> and the sum of the rest of the arguments.  Note that <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">k</Font> must be turned into a list to be used with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> (and most other) commands.  The difference is the number of objects that are discarded.  If this is negative, the procedure will raise an error.  Otherwise, it becomes the final <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRIm1GJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRictRi82I1EhRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRIm1GJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRictRi82I1EhRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> in the Theorem 4 formula.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L864" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here is the implementation.  Note that we use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:map" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">map</Font></Hyperlink> command to compute the factorials of each of the integers in the denominator of the formula.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L865" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">distinguishable := proc(n::posint, k::seq(posint))
  local discard, denomList, i;
  if k = NULL then
    return 1;
  end if;
  discard := n - add(i,i=[k]);
  if discard &lt; 0 then
    error &quot;n must be at least the sum of the rest.&quot;
  end if;
  denomList := [k,discard];
  denomList := map(factorial,denomList);
  return n!/mul(i,i=denomList);
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L867" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">With this procedure, we can compute the number of ways to deal five cards to each of four players from a deck of 52 cards as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L868" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">distinguishable(52,5,5,5,5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IjpTVS4/U2t2TSVHRXk5</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L870" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Indistinguishable Objects and Distinguishable Boxes</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L871" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The text describes the correspondence between questions about placing indistinguishable objects into distinguishable boxes and about combination with repetition questions.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L872" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Example 9 asks how many ways 10 indistinguishable balls can be placed in 8 bins.  We can use the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">numbcombrep</Font> function written earlier.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L873" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numbcombrep(8,10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiZbJT4=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L875" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">It may seem that the arguments were reversed.  Keep in mind that the connection to combinations with repetition is that you are selecting 10 bins from the 8 available bins with repetition allowed.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L920" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Compositions and weak compositions</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L876" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We can also use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,composition" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">composition</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,numbcomp" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numbcomp</Font></Hyperlink> commands to answer questions of this kind.  A <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-composition of a positive integer <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is a way of writing <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> as the sum of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> positive integers where the order of the summands matters.  For example, 4 has three distinct 2-compositions: <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L879" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">A weak composition is similar, but the terms in the sum are allowed to be 0.  Thus 4 has five distinct weak 2-compositions: <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> in addition to the three listed before. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1286" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that the weak <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-compositions of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> correspond to the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-compositions of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  For suppose that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is a weak <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEickYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-composition.  Then each <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImlGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImlGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> is nonnegative.  So </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1288" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>,</Text-field>
